#Choosing the Editor
Any text editor will work. 

- Notepad
	- Least efficient.
	- Teaches spacing the hard way

- Notepad ++
	- Syntax highlighting
	- Provides columns for easier Indentation

- Git Repos
	- Version controlled storage space for files/change history
	- Enables collaboration/efficient code management
	
- VS Code Integrated Development Enviroment (IDE)
	- Free/open source
	- Syntax highlighting, integrates with git, plug-in architecture

- GitHub Copilot
	- Integrate into VS Code
	- Cost per month
	- Vastly improved efficiency

#First Playbook

-- New directory
	-- New file (first-playbook.yml)
------Start of file------------
---
- name: Write a simple file
  hosts: LL-rocky9-01 //or all
  gather_facts: false
  tasks:
    - name: Write file test.txt to temp directory
      ansible.builtin.lineinfile:
        path: /tmp/test.txt
        line: just a quick and simple test
        create: true
-------End of File-------------
		- Save the file
	- Your playbook is ready 

# Create Inventory

- Before you run the playbook, you will need to create an Inventory. 
	-- What is Inventory?
		It just a file wit a big list of hosts against which the Automations
		are supposed to run. 

-- Create new file
	-- new file (inventory)
---------Start of File-----------
[rocky9] //Group name of hosts
LL-rocky9-01 ansible_host=10.0.50.43  //use of magic variable

[rocky9:vars]
ansible_user=ansible
ansible_ssh_pass=password	//This practice is only recommended for Lab enviroment
ansible_become_pass=password	//not a valid practice for prod enviroment. 

---------End of File-----------
Note* Never enter the user pass in any Inventory file. This is only for personal,
	or lab envorment, where the vm's are only for test and destroyed alter. 

# Key versus password authentication

-- Password Authentication
	- Can be simpler to use
	- More Familiar
	- Can be less secure

-- SSH Key Authentication
	- More Secure
	- Less vulnerable
	- Faster once set up
	- More difficult initial config
	- Pubic key added to each host

-- Privilege Esclation
	- Command accounts are user and admin
	- Users can esclate privilge to perform admin
	- Linux: usually "sudo"
	- Windows: "run as administrator"
	- EL host can be added to "wheel" group for sudo permissions

-- Password-Based Caveat
	- Default wants previous SSH key fingerprint for each host
	- Greatly inhibits connecting to new hosts
	- Less secure, so exercise caution

Optional: 
	-- create new file(ansible.cfg)
--------------start of file------------
[defaults]
host_key_check = False

--------------end of file-------------

Note* This is optional, and caution are recommended when following


# Run a playbook

-- Open a new terminal in VS code
-- change to directory where the files are present
-- ansible-playbook -i inventory first-playbook.yml
	-- You will see the output
	-- Observe the output with colour and change. 
-- ansible-playbook -i inventory -u ansible --ask-pass first-playbook.yml
		-- You will be prompted for ssh password for hosts
		-- Enter the password, to complete the automation
-- ansible-playbook -i inventory -u ansible --ask-pass first-playbook.yml --ask-become-pass
		-- In privielge esclation required you have to use this for password. 
		-- It is becoming a new way for ansible use

Note: Most ansible module will make the change only when necessary. 
	A feature called idompotency.


